Cloud computing moves data/workloads to a remote location outside the organization's direct control, introducing new risk categories:
  
  - Security and privacy risks: data breaches (from misconfiguration, unpatched software), compliance issues (regulated industries like
    healthcare, government, finance must know where data resides and how it's encrypted/protected), and insider threats at the provider's
    location.
  - Organizational challenges: service downtime (outside the client's control), and vendor lock-in (differences in underlying infrastructure
    make migrating between providers difficult as cloud presence grows).
  - Cost and performance issues: elastic scaling can cause unexpected costs; static/stable workloads are often cheaper on local servers, while
    variable workloads suit elastic cloud environments; distance from the provider introduces latency.
  
  Mitigation strategies: security hardening (patching, correct firewall/gateway configuration), multi-cloud (reduces vendor lock-in) and
  hybrid cloud (balances cost/control), continuous automated monitoring, proactive incident response planning, and reviewing service
  agreements/compliance before migrating.

Cloud Performance Troubleshooting
  
  Key performance factors: compute resources (CPU/RAM/GPU), network latency/bandwidth, storage read/write performance, and application-level
  performance (queries, caching).
  
  Monitoring tools: Amazon CloudWatch, Azure Monitor, Google Cloud Operations (formerly Stackdriver) — track metrics in real time and
  trigger alerts on thresholds.
  
  Optimization approaches:
    
    - Compute: autoscaling, right-sizing VM specs to the workload type (web servers need network capacity more than storage speed; database
      servers need RAM/disk more than network). Reserved instances suit stable workloads; spot instances suit dynamic ones.
    - Network: CDNs (content delivery networks) reduce latency by placing data copies closer to users; distribute traffic across multiple
      connections to avoid bottlenecks; newer protocols like HTTP/2 improve throughput.
    - Storage: match the storage tier (HDD vs. SSD) to the application's needs; use caching (RAM-based, e.g., Redis, Memcached) for
      frequently accessed data; optimize database indexing/queries.
    
  Favor proactive strategies (continuous monitoring, alerting, automated scaling) over reactive troubleshooting, balanced against cost.

Restoring VMs from Snapshots (Demo — Hyper-V)
  
  In newer Hyper-V Manager versions, "snapshot" = checkpoint. Two checkpoint types (configured under VM Settings → Checkpoints):
    
    - Standard checkpoint: captures a snapshot of RAM/memory contents (what's currently running), not saved disk changes.
    - Production checkpoint: uses the Volume Shadow Copy Service (VSS) to capture the actual disk contents; takes longer to create.
    
  Restoring: right-click the checkpoint → Apply (optionally creating a new checkpoint of the current state first, as a safety net before
  reverting — this cannot be undone once applied). Applying a Standard checkpoint automatically reboots/reconnects the VM; applying a
  Production checkpoint turns the VM off and requires manually starting it again.

VM Performance Improvement

VM performance = speed/efficiency of task execution. Key indicators: CPU utilization (sustained >80% is a concern, but check for memory
swapping first, which drives CPU up artificially), memory usage (watch for load spikes from data caching), disk I/O (tier-dependent), and
network throughput.

Optimization strategies:
  
  - CPU: avoid over-provisioning virtual CPUs beyond actual workload needs or beyond what the host physically has; leverage Turbo Boost and
    Hyper-Threading; use load balancing to spread demand across servers.
  - Memory: Transparent Page Sharing (TPS) deduplicates identical memory pages across VMs to free RAM; use memory reservations for critical
    apps intolerant of swapping; use dynamic memory allocation for more flexible workloads.
  - Disk/storage: choose the right tier (SSD vs. HDD vs. NAS vs. SAN); use fixed-size disks instead of dynamic ones to avoid fragmentation.
  - Proactive management: continuous monitoring and alerting, load balancing, capacity planning (anticipating seasonal/demand surges), and
    regular stress testing. Cloud Performance Management and Optimization

Cloud billing is usage-based (often to the minute), so performance management is tightly linked to cost management.
  
  Key optimization areas: right-sizing compute resources, matching storage tier to workload (SSD for fast analysis, HDD for
  archiving/warehousing), caching frequently used data, replicating relatively static data across locations for faster retrieval, and
  compressing infrequently accessed data to save costs.
  
  Network performance: reduce latency by hosting resources closer to users (via distributed/regional deployments), manage bandwidth
  usage (e.g., scheduling replication outside peak hours), and use security configurations that also reduce unwanted traffic load.
  
  Testing/benchmarking: load testing simulates production-level requests to identify weak components; latency (delay) and throughput
  (data volume transmitted) are core network metrics to evaluate; industry and internal historical benchmarks help set performance
  expectations. Automated performance/auditing tools help continuously rebalance cost vs. performance, since cloud environments are
  elastic and constantly shifting.

VM Backup Security

A VM backup is a complete copy of the OS, applications, and data — anyone who gains access to it has everything. Risks of unsecured backups:
unauthorized access/copying, corruption (including ransomware/malware), and potential violation of regulatory compliance if backups are
stored outside a secured network.

Core security principles:
  
  - Encryption: both backup files at rest and data in transit should be encrypted, ideally with AES-256 (data at rest) and TLS/SSL
    (data in transit). Use centralized key management to protect and rotate encryption keys.
  - Access control: Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) limits access to the minimum needed; multi-factor authentication (MFA) adds identity
    assurance.
  - Data immutability: once written, backup data cannot be changed — a key defense against ransomware, since an unaltered backup copy lets
    you restore without paying a ransom.
  - Monitoring/auditing: track who accessed backup data and when.

Relevant compliance regulations: GDPR (EU personal data), HIPAA (US health information), SOC 2 (general data-handling controls).

Best practices: store backups on dedicated storage separate from the production VM's disk (never on the same hardware); use air-gapped
backups (no network connection) for extra protection; log and monitor the backup process itself (who made it, when, where it's stored, who
accessed it); keep multiple copies in different locations/types; and regularly test restoring backups (not just creating them) — including
restoring to a separate test environment. Remember: a snapshot only records changes since the last snapshot and is not a substitute for a
complete/traditional backup — use both together.

Virtual Network Troubleshooting
  
  - Virtual networks (software-defined networks, SDNs) share some issues with physical networks but also have unique, cloud-specific ones.
  
  - Key components: virtual switches/routers, virtual NICs (configured in the hypervisor, tied to a switch/port group), IP addressing
    (often via DHCP configured in the hypervisor), firewalls (usually part of the guest OS) vs. network security groups (part of the
    virtual network itself), and SDN controllers (define traffic paths for virtual routers).
  
  - Common issues: connectivity failures (bad routing, firewall misconfiguration), performance degradation (under-provisioned hardware or
    traffic spikes), security issues (unnecessary traffic, misconfigured rules enabling DoS), and DNS/DHCP misconfigurations
    (wrong destinations or unreachable hosts).
  
  - Diagnostic tools: cloud-native — AWS VPC Flow Logs, Azure Network Watcher, Google Cloud VPC Diagnostics. Command-line — ping,
    traceroute (connectivity via ICMP), netstat, tcpdump, Wireshark (traffic/configuration analysis). Hypervisor-specific — VMware NSX
    troubleshooting utilities, Hyper-V Virtual Switch Manager diagnostics.
  
  - Best practices: isolate the problem (hardware vs. software/cloud), use substitution (swap known-good components), verify configurations
    (IP, DNS, firewall, NAT, VPN), check logs, use network segmentation and load balancing to relieve congestion, and follow zero trust
    principles (no implicit access; every action requires explicit, non-transferable permission).

Cloud Monitoring and Logging
  
  Purpose: track system health, performance, and security events in a constantly changing (elastic) cloud environment, enabling proactive
  issue detection and compliance.
  
  Three core components:
  
  - Monitoring: real-time tracking of metrics (CPU, memory, network, storage), often shown as graphs/dashboards.
  - Logging: recording historical system/security events (configuration changes, errors, logons) for later analysis and compliance proof.
  - Alerting: automated notifications when thresholds/anomalies are detected, reducing the need for constant manual monitoring.
  
  Key metrics tracked: compute (CPU, memory, instance availability, autoscaling thresholds), network (latency, packet loss, bandwidth,
  VPC flow logs), and storage/database (read/write speed, disk I/O, query performance, cache hit/miss ratio).
  
  Best practices: centralize log collection (simplifies analysis, backup, and compliance retention), configure alerts for abnormal activity
  with a defined escalation/response plan, regularly test that alerts actually work, and conduct post-incident reviews to improve procedures.
  Future trends: AI-driven anomaly detection, predictive analytics, and automated compliance remediation tools.

Quick Exam-Focus Reminders
  
  - Vendor lock-in = difficulty migrating away from a cloud provider due to infrastructure differences; multi-cloud strategy helps
    mitigate it.
  - Standard checkpoint (Hyper-V) = RAM/memory snapshot; Production checkpoint = disk snapshot via VSS (Volume Shadow Copy Service).
  - A snapshot only records changes since the last snapshot — it is NOT a replacement for a full/traditional backup.
  - VM backup core security principles: encryption (AES-256 at rest, TLS/SSL in transit), RBAC + MFA for access control, and data
    immutability (key ransomware defense).
  - Compliance standards to recognize: GDPR (EU), HIPAA (US health data), SOC 2 (data controls).
  - Best backup practice: store backups on separate/dedicated storage from the production VM; consider air-gapped backups; always test
    restores, not just backups.
  - CDN = reduces latency by caching data geographically closer to users.
  - Reserved instances = stable/predictable workloads; spot instances = dynamic/interruptible workloads (cheaper, less reliable).
  - Network security groups = part of the virtual network; firewalls = typically part of the guest OS — know the distinction.
  - Diagnostic tools to recognize: ping/traceroute (connectivity, ICMP), netstat/tcpdump/Wireshark (traffic/config analysis), plus
    cloud-native tools (AWS VPC Flow Logs, Azure Network Watcher, Google Cloud VPC Diagnostics).
  - Zero trust = no implicit access; every action requires explicit, non-transferable permission.
  - Monitoring vs. logging vs. alerting: monitoring = real-time tracking; logging = historical record; alerting = automated notification on
    thresholds/anomalies.
